<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">San'in region</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The San'in region is the part of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a>, comprising today's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tottori">Tottori</a> and Shimane prefectures and the north of Yamaguchi. Its name, 'the shady side of the mountains', contrasts it with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanyo-region">San'yō region</a> on the Inland Sea. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a> it was dominated first by the Amago and then by the Mōri clans.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sanin-region.jpg" alt="San'in region" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Nature Box · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:San%27in_region_of_Japan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The San'in region is the northern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chugoku-region">Chūgoku region</a> of Honshū, a long coastline on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a> that sweeps south to the Chūgoku Mountains. It is primarily mountainous with few plains. Its climate is less harsh than that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokuriku-region">Hokuriku region</a> to the north, but its winters bring the heavy snow and rainfall typical of the Japan Sea coast. The northern areas of Hyōgo and Kyōto prefectures are sometimes counted as part of it as well.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region has numerous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleolithic">Paleolithic</a> and Jōmon remains, and the Mukibanda Yayoi remains in the foothills of Mount Daisen, in the cities of Daisen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yonago">Yonago</a>, are the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>; though only partly excavated, they indicate that the San'in was then a regional centre of power. Much of the mythology of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a> is set in the Izumo area, home to Izumo-taisha, one of the oldest and most important Shinto shrines, and eastern Shimane had early ties with the Asian mainland.</p><p>In the Asuka period the region became the San'indō, one of the five provinces and seven circuits of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ritsuryo">Ritsuryō</a> legal system. The name meant both the area, the provinces of Tanba, Tango, Tajima, Inaba, Hōki, Izumo, Iwami and Oki, and the main road that linked it to the capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. The road carried goods and served armies, and it was most important in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> fixed its route and shukuba post stations and the daimyō used it for their sankin-kōtai journeys to Edo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The San'in has no administrative authority. Japan Route 9, the San'in Expressway and the JR West San'in Main Line follow the old San'indō, along which remains of the post stations survive. The cities of more than 100,000 people are the prefectural capitals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tottori">Tottori</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsue">Matsue</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yonago">Yonago</a> and Izumo, and the region's coasts and mountains are protected as national, prefectural and municipal parks.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “San'in region” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tsunehisa and his grandson Haruhisa extended their power over the <b>San'in</b> and San'yo regions, at their height claiming influence over eleven provinces.</blockquote><a href="/state/amago-clan-domain">Amago clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
